 ## 8. Conclusion
 
-I have argued that Flattening fails for counterfactuals. The evidence is a bare instance of its rule form, (1)–(2), together with a control that changes nothing but counterfactual dependence. Every unnested counterfactual about who came is held fixed across the two cases, as are the exclusive, additive and universal construals. Only the scenario-relative reading predicts the contrast, and Flattening fails on that reading. A persistent shift of context fails the persistence test. A local one is the scenario-relative interpretation under another name. The Buttons rest the inner verdict on locality. Theorem 1, a short consequence of CEM and either half of Flattening, extends the point to symmetric cases. The argument depends on judgments about nested counterfactuals, as any argument about Flattening must, and I have said how to test them. It leaves indicatives, 4 and H open, and it explains why Dorr and Mandelkern's test pairs did not reveal the failure.
+The matched cases isolate a proposed failure of Flattening under scenario-relative interpretation. Their finite models agree on all first-degree counterfactuals while separating the nested sentence from its flattening partner. The control removes several simple alternative readings; Buttons adds a conditional-selection argument based on local changes; and Theorem 1 displays the associated commitment of either half of Flattening within C2.
+
+The decisive outstanding issue is interpretation. The formal models do not prove that ordinary English expresses their selection functions. Persistent shifts face the stated discourse test, while temporary contextual shifts and rejection of the proposed nested judgment remain possible. The contribution is therefore a structured challenge and an explicit comparison between predictions, not a completed empirical case against sequence semantics. If the stable scenario-relative reading is sustained, Flattening cannot be a law of that reading of the counterfactual. If it is not, the natural-language objection fails while the mathematical separation remains.
 
